---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Nolan's Workshop"
  description="Nolan's Workshop: woodworking projects, shop systems, jigs, work surfaces, finishing, tools, and build lessons."
>
  <header class="page-header">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">Nolan's Workshop</p>
      <h1>Build it to be used.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        The physical-build side of the site: woodworking, shop systems, jigs, work surfaces, finishing, and the practical decisions that turn a sketch into something that earns space in the shop.
      </p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="section section--warm">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Workshop identity</p>
          <h2>One umbrella. A distinct maker brand.</h2>
        </div>
        <p class="section-intro">
          Nolan's Workshop is the name used for the woodworking side of my work, including a physical wood brand. It lives inside Unyielding Claw for now rather than creating a second site that has to be maintained separately.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="workshop-grid">
        <article class="workshop-card">
          <h3>Shop systems & jigs</h3>
          <p>Purpose-built fixtures, sleds, stands, storage, workholding, and shop improvements that make the next project easier or safer.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="workshop-card">
          <h3>Work surfaces & furniture</h3>
          <p>Benches, tables, tops, and utility builds where structure, mobility, finish, and actual use matter more than showroom perfection.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="workshop-card">
          <h3>Small builds & experiments</h3>
          <p>Projects that are worth documenting because of the process: routing, joinery, finishing, material choices, mistakes, and revisions.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell split-feature">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">How I document builds</p>
        <h2>The useful part is usually the decision.</h2>
        <p class="section-intro">
          Future project pages will focus on what the build had to do, what constraints shaped it, what I owned already, what I changed after testing, and what I would do differently—not just a gallery of finished photos.
        </p>
      </div>
      <aside class="feature-note">
        <strong>Plans are not automatically open source.</strong>
        <p>Photos, drawings, measurements, plans, written instructions, and the Nolan's Workshop identity remain all rights reserved unless a specific project says otherwise. Individual plans can be licensed deliberately when there is a reason.</p>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--dark">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">The crossover</p>
          <h2>Same habits. Different material.</h2>
        </div>
        <p class="section-intro">
          A good jig and a good software tool share more than they first appear to: narrow purpose, predictable behavior, respect for constraints, and enough simplicity that the tool does not become the project.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
